WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.240
Okay.

00:04.240 --> 00:06.280
So now let's talk about aggregations.

00:06.280 --> 00:13.120
And here the tip says use Columnstore index for aggregations on large tables like for example fact tables.

00:13.120 --> 00:16.160
And that's because Columnstore index is going to compress the data.

00:16.160 --> 00:21.760
So the size of the data going to be smaller and as will the aggregation is super fast because we are

00:21.760 --> 00:25.920
selecting only the relevant informations, only the relevant columns.

00:25.920 --> 00:29.600
So it makes it a perfect setup for aggregating large tables.

00:29.640 --> 00:33.720
And now let's say that we have hundreds of millions of orders and we have this query over here.

00:33.720 --> 00:39.520
So the best practice says convert this table to a clustered Columnstore index.

00:39.520 --> 00:45.800
So if you go and create this clustered index over here, the whole table can have amazing performance

00:46.000 --> 00:47.560
for aggregations like this.

00:47.560 --> 00:48.120
All right.

00:48.120 --> 00:54.200
So to the next one it says Pre-aggregate data and store it in a new table for reporting.

00:54.200 --> 00:58.920
So let's say that we have like a big query where we are aggregating the data and so on.

00:58.920 --> 01:03.280
And this query takes really long time let's say like five minutes or something like that.

01:03.280 --> 01:08.530
But now the problem with that I would like to show the results as a report, maybe to my manager or

01:08.570 --> 01:10.050
let's say during a meeting.

01:10.090 --> 01:14.570
It's going to be really bad if everyone have to wait until the query is done.

01:14.650 --> 01:20.210
So the best practice here, if you have like a query that runs very slow, what you can do, you can

01:20.210 --> 01:23.170
go and store the results in a table.

01:23.170 --> 01:29.850
So if I go over here and say into sales summary, what can happen is going to store the result inside

01:29.850 --> 01:30.370
this table.

01:30.370 --> 01:31.930
So let's go and execute it.

01:31.930 --> 01:35.010
And now with that we have a nice table where everything is prepared.

01:35.010 --> 01:38.610
So all we have to do is to go and query this table.

01:38.610 --> 01:42.130
And of course it's going to be very fast because it's only select statements.

01:42.130 --> 01:48.050
And with that you have like prepared and pre-aggregated the data to have like fast reports.

01:48.050 --> 01:49.210
So don't forget about this.

01:49.210 --> 01:54.650
If you have a BigQuery, you can insert the result of this query in a new table in order later to use

01:54.650 --> 01:55.610
it for reporting.

01:55.770 --> 02:00.410
But one thing that you have to make sure that you have always to update this table.

02:00.410 --> 02:04.610
So if we have a new orders, it will not be presented inside the sales summary.

02:04.850 --> 02:10.050
You have to go and run this query again in order to get a new data inside the sales summary.

02:10.050 --> 02:14.770
So those are the tips on how to improve the performance of your aggregations in your SQL.
